10:13:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: We *can* make new elements with particle accelerators, and that is how many of the heaviest known elements were discovered—such as neptunium, plutonium, einsteinium, and elements 113 through 118. However, knowing or predicting an element’s properties does not mean it is easy to produce. The main limitations are nuclear physics: - **You must build the right nucleus.** An element is defined by its number of protons. To make a new one, scientists must collide nuclei so they fuse into a nucleus with the desired proton number. - **Nuclei strongly repel each other.** Both nuclei are positively charged, so they electrically repel before getting close enough for the strong nuclear force to fuse them. Accelerators provide energy to overcome this, but too much energy can cause the combined nucleus to break apart instead. - **Successful fusion is extremely rare.** For superheavy elements, researchers may fire trillions of ions at a target and create only a few atoms over weeks or months. - **The new atom may decay almost instantly.** Very heavy nuclei often undergo radioactive decay in milliseconds, microseconds, or less. Even if made, there may be no time to collect a visible sample or study ordinary chemical properties. - **Targets and beams are difficult to obtain.** Many experiments require rare, radioactive target materials, such as berkelium or californium, which are hard to make in useful amounts. - **Predicted properties are uncertain.** We can estimate nuclear stability and chemical behavior, but predictions become less reliable for elements beyond those already observed. Tiny nuclear-structure differences can determine whether a nucleus lasts years, seconds, or effectively no time at all. So particle accelerators can make new elements, but usually only atom by atom and for very short times. Making enough of a new superheavy element to hold, see, or use in a material is generally far beyond current capabilities.
