10:13:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a very common school-level simplification. An **element** is not defined by whether it occurs naturally. It is defined by the number of **protons** in its atomic nucleus: - 1 proton = hydrogen - 6 protons = carbon - 26 protons = iron - 79 protons = gold - 92 protons = uranium If you make a nucleus with, say, 95 protons, it is americium—whether it formed in a star, a nuclear reactor, an explosion, or a particle accelerator. Nature made most elements through stellar processes: - The early universe made mostly hydrogen and helium. - Stars fuse lighter elements into heavier ones, up to roughly iron. - Supernovae, neutron-star mergers, and other violent events make many heavier elements. - Radioactive decay can turn one element into another over time. But humans can also change one element into another. This is called **nuclear transmutation**. It is different from chemistry: chemical reactions rearrange electrons and molecules, while nuclear reactions alter the nucleus itself. For example, nuclear reactors produce plutonium from uranium through neutron capture and radioactive decay. Particle accelerators can smash or fuse nuclei to make isotopes and elements that are rare or absent in nature. Some elements are essentially human-made in practice. Technetium (43) and promethium (61), for example, have no stable isotopes and occur naturally only in tiny trace amounts; most of the amounts we use are produced artificially. Elements beyond uranium are called **transuranium elements**, and many were first made in reactors or accelerators. So the periodic table is not a catalog of “things found lying around in nature.” It is a catalog of the possible kinds of atoms, organized by proton number. Some kinds are abundant and stable; some are rare; and some can exist only briefly when we create them in a machine.
